LENGTHCSTl(G)J-FJ ; F=INDEX(STl(O),• 'J ; END ; F=INOEXCSllC0) 9 1 ,) 1 ); IF F\u003eO THEN s T lC 0 , = su 8 ~ T R ( s 1 1 ( tJ ) t l t F- u I I SUBSTRCSTlCOJ,F+l,LENGTHfSTl(O))-FJ ; IX=IX+l • IF IX\u003el fHEN SllC0)= 1 +'11STUO) ; PUT SKIP LIST CST110)) ; EA: END ; IF IX=O THEN PUT SKIP Li~T ( 1 0 1 ) ; FREE STl ; FREE NUl ; FREE NUE 1 ; GO TO STA ; OUTT: END ; ","id":229066012,"identifiers":{"doi":null,"oai":"oai:scholarsmine.mst.edu:doctoral_dissertations-3090"},"title":"Structure of zero divisors, and other algebraic structures, in higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebras","language":{"code":"en","name":"English"},"publishedDate":"1972-01-01T08:00:00+00:00","publisher":"Scholars\\u27 Mine","references":[],"sourceFulltextUrls":["https://scholarsmine.mst.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=3090\u0026context=doctoral_dissertations"],"updatedDate":"","yearPublished":"1972","links":[{"type":"download","url":"https://core.ac.uk/download/229066012.pdf"},{"type":"reader","url":"https://core.ac.uk/reader/229066012"},{"type":"thumbnail_m","url":"https://core.ac.uk/image/229066012/medium"},{"type":"thumbnail_l","url":"https://core.ac.uk/image/229066012/large"},{"type":"display","url":"https://core.ac.uk/outputs/229066012"}],"abstract":"Real Cayley-Dickson algebras are a class of 2ⁿ-dimensional real algebras containing the real numbers, complex numbers, quaternions, and the octonions (Cayley numbers) as special cases. Each real Cayley-Dickson algebra of dimension greater than eight (a higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebra) is a real normed algebra containing a multiplicative identity and an inverse for each nonzero element. In addition, each element a in the algebra has defined for it a conjugate element ā analogous to the conjugate in the complex numbers. These algebras are not alternative, but are flexible and satisfy the noncommutative Jordan identity. Each element in these algebras can be written A= a₁+ea₂ where e is a basis element and a₁,a₂ are elements of the Cayley-Dickson algebra of next lower dimension. Results include the facts that for each real Cayley-Dickson algebra aⁱaʲ = aⁱ⁺ʲ and (aⁱb)aʲ = aⁱ(baʲ) for all integers i,j and any a,b in the algebra. The major result concerns zero divisors.... --Abstract, page ii","tags":["text","Cayley algebrasOrdered algebraic structuresDivisor theoryNoncommutative algebras","Mathematics"],"fulltextStatus":"disabled","subjects":["text"],"oai":"oai:scholarsmine.mst.edu:doctoral_dissertations-3090","deleted":"ALLOWED","disabled":false,"journals":null,"repositories":{"id":"12679","openDoarId":0,"name":"Missouri University of Science and Technology (Missouri S\u0026T): Scholars' Mine","urlHomepage":null,"uriJournals":null,"physicalName":"noname","roarId":0,"baseId":0,"pdfStatus":null,"nrUpdates":0,"lastUpdateTime":null},"repositoryDocument":{"id":229066012,"depositedDate":"","publishedDate":"1972-01-01T08:00:00+00:00","updatedDate":"2025-10-04T18:46:46+01:00","acceptedDate":"","createdDate":"2019-10-17T20:10:32+01:00"},"urls":["https://scholarsmine.mst.edu/doctoral_dissertations/2088","https://scholarsmine.mst.edu/context/doctoral_dissertations/article/3090/viewcontent/Brown_Harmon_1972.pdf"],"lastUpdate":"2025-10-04T18:46:46+01:00","setSpecs":[],"license":"","orcids":[],"sdg":null,"structuredData":"{\"@context\":\"http://schema.org\",\"@graph\":[{\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"item\":{\"@id\":\"https://core.ac.uk\",\"name\":\"CORE\"}},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"item\":{\"@id\":\"https://core.ac.uk/search?q=repositories.id:(12679)\",\"name\":\"Missouri University of Science and Technology (Missouri S\u0026T): Scholars' Mine\"}},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"item\":{\"@id\":\"https://core.ac.uk/reader/229066012\",\"name\":\"Structure of zero divisors, and other algebraic structures, in higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebras\",\"image\":\"https://core.ac.uk/image/229066012/large\"}}]},{\"@type\":\"ScholarlyArticle\",\"@id\":\"https://core.ac.uk/reader/229066012\",\"headline\":\"Structure of zero divisors, and other algebraic structures, in higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebras\",\"description\":\"Real Cayley-Dickson algebras are a class of 2ⁿ-dimensional real algebras containing the real numbers, complex numbers, quaternions, and the octonions (Cayley numbers) as special cases. Each real Cayley-Dickson algebra of dimension greater than eight (a higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebra) is a real normed algebra containing a multiplicative identity and an inverse for each nonzero element. In addition, each element a in the algebra has defined for it a conjugate element ā analogous to the conjugate in the complex numbers. These algebras are not alternative, but are flexible and satisfy the noncommutative Jordan identity. Each element in these algebras can be written A= a₁+ea₂ where e is a basis element and a₁,a₂ are elements of the Cayley-Dickson algebra of next lower dimension. Results include the facts that for each real Cayley-Dickson algebra aⁱaʲ = aⁱ⁺ʲ and (aⁱb)aʲ = aⁱ(baʲ) for all integers i,j and any a,b in the algebra. The major result concerns zero divisors.... --Abstract, page ii\",\"name\":\"Structure of zero divisors, and other algebraic structures, in higher dimensional real Cayley-Dickson algebras\",\"author\":[{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":{\"name\":\"Brown, Harmon Caril\"}}],\"datePublished\":\"\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":true,\"provider\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Missouri University of Science and Technology (Missouri S\u0026T): Scholars' Mine\"},\"image\":\"https://core.ac.uk/image/229066012/large\",\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Scholars\\\\u27 Mine\"}}]}","statusCode":200},"__N_SSP":true},"page":"/[pdfId]","query":{"pdfId":"229066012"},"buildId":"VfMvJDL3FGOobDubx0n6N","assetPrefix":"/reader","isFallback":false,"gssp":true}</script><script nomodule="" src="/reader/_next/static/chun